5.116.m:
Having duly prayed to Varuṇa, Rāghava bowed to Śambhu and Vasiṣṭha.
Having received blessings from them, with his lotus-feet washed by Hanūmat, he went to his resting-place;
he then offered oblations into the fires and, praised by bards and panegyrists, came outside.

Young girls too, whose slender limbs were as brilliant as flashes of lightning, whose chests were adorned with various necklaces over bud-like lotus-breasts just bursting forth, speaking softly and walking with extremely playful, gentle steps, and elderly women arrived.

Then, just one krośa before [the capital of] Videha, in a mango grove, which had various spread-out branches and regions where young wild deer pricked up their ears

where diverse birds rested in regions adorned with high and low mansions built of silver; where rows of trees were graced by the meditation and worship of groups of matted-haired ascetics whose bodies were wrapped in golden tree-bark and smeared with ashes;

[in that mango grove] where rows of trees were graced by the meditation and worship of groups of matted-haired ascetics whose bodies were wrapped in golden tree-bark and smeared with ashes;

which possessed lakes whose moving ripples were overwhelmed by the weight of the breasts of the wives of various Vidyādharas;

whose every quarter was perfumed with the fragrance of blossoms of various colors; where courtesans, with bodies radiant with splendor, cast wide tremulous eyes resembling flashing saphara-fish out of a desire for love-play here and there;

—in that [grove] filled with various wonders, Daśaratha dwelt happily together with his ministers, purohita, and sons headed by delightful Rāma.

beautified with diverse mansions, gateway towers, pleasure gardens, and temples of deities; crowded with young women clever in mutual playful amours; having a great water-booth made of uśīra grass; its streets graced by playfully joyful people;

its highways adorned with diverse commodities; its monasteries here and there resounding with the chanting of the Veda; having expositions of Mīmāṃsā and other disciplines and chanting of the Sāmas in every house;

having Brahmin quarters fragrant with the scent of very sacred sacrificial oblations and resonant with the tones, words, krama-recitation, and Śruti of the Sāmaveda and other Vedas; with costumes of the adhvaryu priests perfumed with agallochum and saffron, brought at great expense to the houses of many lords; having passionate women with soft garments and lips reddened by betel;

graced by passionate women dressed in soft garments, their lips reddened by betel, speaking soft and sharp words, and giving replies indicated by gestures of the hands;

adorned by people bearing hands laden with various gifts and offering replies accompanied by subtle gestures; whose women had their delicate

white hips wrapped in garments, the upper part of which was draped over the middle of their glossy, round breasts rubbing against one another,

Then at night, sighing repeatedly, although sleepy, he found no sleep.

Then, in the middle of the night, having purified himself, the king [beheld] Tryambaka, accompanied by Ambikā, wearing auspicious silk,

by all the gods headed by Śakra, by the foremost sages headed by Bhṛgu, by the gandharvas headed by Hāhā and Tumburu, both Śruti and Smṛti...

and by the hosts of Siddhas, Vidyādharas, and Mātṛkās, and by the attendants headed by Nandī;

who removes all inauspiciousness; whose head is attended by the Ganges with its holy waters and by the unblemished Moon;

who receives with a smile, desire, and an affectionate glance the betel roll offered by Girijā seated on his left lap; white as cow's milk, with a throat dark as contrasting musk; wearing a matted knot formed of soft, fine, glossy braids of hair;

whose cheeks were adorned with earrings of pure gold; appearing sixteen years of age;

whose head was bound with an end-cloth of safflower hue bearing thick pearls resembling cow's milk;
